   ddllooppeenn(())
       The  function ddllooppeenn() loads the dynamic shared object (shared library)
       file named by the null‐terminated string _p_a_t_h  and  returns  an  opaque
       "handle"  for  the  loaded  object.  This handle is employed with other
       functions in the dlopen API, such as  ddllssyymm(3),  ddllaaddddrr(3),  ddlliinnffoo(3),
       and ddllcclloossee().

       If  _p_a_t_h is NULL, then the returned handle is for the main program.  If
       _p_a_t_h contains a slash ("/"), then it is interpreted as a  (relative  or
       absolute) pathname.  Otherwise, the dynamic linker searches for the ob‐
       ject as follows (see lldd..ssoo(8) for further details):

       •  (ELF  only)  If the calling object (i.e., the shared library or exe‐
          cutable from which ddllooppeenn() is called) contains a DT_RPATH tag,  and
          does  not  contain  a DT_RUNPATH tag, then the directories listed in
          the DT_RPATH tag are searched.

       •  If, at the time that the program was started, the environment  vari‐
          able  LLDD__LLIIBBRRAARRYY__PPAATTHH  was defined to contain a colon‐separated list
          of directories, then these are searched.  (As  a  security  measure,
          this variable is ignored for set‐user‐ID and set‐group‐ID programs.)

       •  (ELF only) If the calling object contains a DT_RUNPATH tag, then the
          directories listed in that tag are searched.

       •  The  cache  file  _/_e_t_c_/_l_d_._s_o_._c_a_c_h_e  (maintained  by  llddccoonnffiigg(8)) is
          checked to see whether it contains an entry for _p_a_t_h.

       •  The directories _/_l_i_b and _/_u_s_r_/_l_i_b are searched (in that order).

       If the object specified by _p_a_t_h has dependencies on  other  shared  ob‐
       jects,  then  these are also automatically loaded by the dynamic linker
       using the same rules.  (This process may occur  recursively,  if  those
       objects in turn have dependencies, and so on.)

       One of the following two values must be included in _f_l_a_g_s:

       RRTTLLDD__LLAAZZYY
              Perform  lazy  binding.   Resolve  symbols only as the code that
              references them is executed.  If the symbol is never referenced,
              then it is never resolved.  (Lazy binding is performed only  for
              function  references; references to variables are always immedi‐
              ately bound when the shared  object  is  loaded.)   Since  glibc
              2.1.1,  this flag is overridden by the effect of the LLDD__BBIINNDD__NNOOWW
              environment variable.

       RRTTLLDD__NNOOWW
              If  this  value  is  specified,  or  the  environment   variable
              LLDD__BBIINNDD__NNOOWW  is  set to a nonempty string, all undefined symbols
              in the shared object are resolved before ddllooppeenn()  returns.   If
              this cannot be done, an error is returned.

       Zero or more of the following values may also be ORed in _f_l_a_g_s:

       RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL
              The symbols defined by this shared object will be made available
              for symbol resolution of subsequently loaded shared objects.

       RRTTLLDD__LLOOCCAALL
              This  is the converse of RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL, and the default if neither
              flag is specified.  Symbols defined in this  shared  object  are
              not  made available to resolve references in subsequently loaded
              shared objects.

       RRTTLLDD__NNOODDEELLEETTEE (since glibc 2.2)
              Do not unload the shared object during ddllcclloossee().  Consequently,
              the object’s static and global variables are  not  reinitialized
              if the object is reloaded with ddllooppeenn() at a later time.

       RRTTLLDD__NNOOLLOOAADD (since glibc 2.2)
              Don’t  load  the shared object.  This can be used to test if the
              object is already resident (ddllooppeenn() returns NULL if it is  not,
              or  the  object’s handle if it is resident).  This flag can also
              be used to promote the flags on a shared object that is  already
              loaded.  For example, a shared object that was previously loaded
              with RRTTLLDD__LLOOCCAALL can be reopened with RRTTLLDD__NNOOLLOOAADD || RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL.

       RRTTLLDD__DDEEEEPPBBIINNDD (since glibc 2.3.4)
              Place  the  lookup  scope  of  the symbols in this shared object
              ahead of the global scope.  This means that a self‐contained ob‐
              ject will use its own symbols in preference  to  global  symbols
              with  the  same name contained in objects that have already been
              loaded.

       If _p_a_t_h is NULL, then the returned handle  is  for  the  main  program.
       When given to ddllssyymm(3), this handle causes a search for a symbol in the
       main program, followed by all shared objects loaded at program startup,
       and   then  all  shared  objects  loaded  by  ddllooppeenn()  with  the  flag
       RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL.

       Symbol references in the shared object are resolved using  (in  order):
       symbols  in the link map of objects loaded for the main program and its
       dependencies; symbols in shared objects (and their  dependencies)  that
       were  previously  opened  with ddllooppeenn() using the RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL flag; and
       definitions in the shared object itself (and any dependencies that were
       loaded for that object).

       Any global symbols in the executable that were placed into its  dynamic
       symbol  table  by lldd(1) can also be used to resolve references in a dy‐
       namically loaded shared object.  Symbols may be placed in  the  dynamic
       symbol  table  either  because  the executable was linked with the flag
       "-rdynamic" (or, synonymously, "--export-dynamic"), which causes all of
       the executable’s global symbols to be placed in the dynamic symbol  ta‐
       ble,  or because lldd(1) noted a dependency on a symbol in another object
       during static linking.

       If the same shared object is opened again with ddllooppeenn(), the  same  ob‐
       ject handle is returned.  The dynamic linker maintains reference counts
       for  object handles, so a dynamically loaded shared object is not deal‐
       located until ddllcclloossee() has been called on it as many times as ddllooppeenn()
       has succeeded on it.  Constructors (see below) are called only when the
       object is actually loaded into memory (i.e., when the  reference  count
       increases to 1).

       A  subsequent  ddllooppeenn()  call  that  loads  the same shared object with
       RRTTLLDD__NNOOWW may force symbol resolution for a shared object earlier loaded
       with RRTTLLDD__LLAAZZYY.  Similarly, an object that was previously  opened  with
       RRTTLLDD__LLOOCCAALL can be promoted to RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL in a subsequent ddllooppeenn().

       If ddllooppeenn() fails for any reason, it returns NULL.

   ddllmmooppeenn(())
       This function performs the same task as ddllooppeenn()—the _p_a_t_h and _f_l_a_g_s ar‐
       guments, as well as the return value, are the same, except for the dif‐
       ferences noted below.

       The  ddllmmooppeenn()  function differs from ddllooppeenn() primarily in that it ac‐
       cepts an additional argument, _l_m_i_d, that specifies  the  link‐map  list
       (also  referred to as a _n_a_m_e_s_p_a_c_e) in which the shared object should be
       loaded.  (By comparison, ddllooppeenn() adds the  dynamically  loaded  shared
       object  to  the  same  namespace  as  the  shared object from which the
       ddllooppeenn() call is made.)  The _L_m_i_d___t  type  is  an  opaque  handle  that
       refers to a namespace.

       The  _l_m_i_d argument is either the ID of an existing namespace (which can
       be obtained using the ddlliinnffoo(3) RRTTLLDD__DDII__LLMMIIDD request)  or  one  of  the
       following special values:

       LLMM__IIDD__BBAASSEE
              Load  the  shared object in the initial namespace (i.e., the ap‐
              plication’s namespace).

       LLMM__IIDD__NNEEWWLLMM
              Create a new namespace and load the shared object in that  name‐
              space.   The object must have been correctly linked to reference
              all of the other shared objects that it requires, since the  new
              namespace is initially empty.

       If _p_a_t_h is NULL, then the only permitted value for _l_m_i_d is LLMM__IIDD__BBAASSEE.

   ddllcclloossee(())
       The  function  ddllcclloossee()  decrements the reference count on the dynami‐
       cally loaded shared object referred to by _h_a_n_d_l_e.

       If the object’s reference count drops to zero and no  symbols  in  this
       object are required by other objects, then the object is unloaded after
       first calling any destructors defined for the object.  (Symbols in this
       object  might  be  required  in  another object because this object was
       opened with the RRTTLLDD__GGLLOOBBAALL flag and one of its symbols satisfied a re‐
       location in another object.)

       All shared objects that were automatically loaded when ddllooppeenn() was in‐
       voked on the object referred to by _h_a_n_d_l_e are recursively closed in the
       same manner.

       A successful return from ddllcclloossee() does not guarantee that the  symbols
       associated with _h_a_n_d_l_e are removed from the caller’s address space.  In
       addition to references resulting from explicit ddllooppeenn() calls, a shared
       object  may have been implicitly loaded (and reference counted) because
       of dependencies in other shared objects.  Only when all references have
       been released can the shared object be removed from the address space.

